export const prerender = true
---

<DocsPage id="skills-and-commands">

          <p>
            A skill is a packaged set of instructions for a recurring kind of work — a house style, a review checklist,
            a deployment runbook. A custom command is a prompt template you keep as a Markdown file.
          </p>

          <h2 id="skills">Skills</h2>
          <p>
            A skill is a folder with a Markdown file named <code>SKILL.md</code>, in capitals, since on Linux the case
            matters. Its frontmatter gives the skill's <code>name</code> and <code>description</code>, and its body
            holds the instructions. Vector reads no other file name, so a skill saved as <code>&lt;name&gt;.md</code>
            is not found, and a <code>SKILL.md</code> without a <code>name</code> is skipped. The name comes from the
            frontmatter, not the folder; give each skill its own, because when two share one Vector logs a warning and
            keeps only one. The agent sees each skill that has a description, by name and description, and loads one
            by name, along with a sample of the files beside it.
          </p>
          <p>Vector looks for <code>SKILL.md</code> files at any depth under these folders:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <code>skill/</code> or <code>skills/</code> in Vector's own config folder (beside the
              <code>AGENTS.md</code> that <a href="/docs/vector-agent#repository-rules">Repository rules</a>
              describes), in a <code>.vector</code> folder in your home folder, and in the repository's
              <code>.vector</code> folders, from the folder you work in up to the repository's root.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code>.claude/skills/</code> and <code>.agents/skills/</code>, in your home folder and in the repository,
              from the folder you work in up to its root. Setting <code>VECTOR_DISABLE_EXTERNAL_SKILLS</code> turns
              these off.
            </li>
            <li>
              Each folder listed in <code>skills.paths</code> in your settings. A path starting with <code>~/</code> is
              in your home folder, and a relative path starts from the folder you work in.
            </li>
            <li>The skills downloaded from each address in <code>skills.urls</code>.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The repository's folders are read only once you trust the repository. A permission rule for
            <code>skill</code>, whose pattern is the skill's name, can keep a skill from an agent: a skill it denies is
            left out of that agent's list.
          </p>
          <p>
            An address in <code>skills.urls</code> serves an <code>index.json</code> that lists its skills and their
            files, and Vector downloads them into its cache, one folder for each address and skill. When the index
            gives a skill a <code>version</code>, a changed version is downloaded whole and replaces the old folder;
            without one, files already downloaded are not fetched again. A downloaded skill too loads only from its
            <code>SKILL.md</code>. Skills are a Vector mechanism, not an MCP server.
          </p>

          <h2 id="commands">Custom commands</h2>
          <p>
            Your own commands are Markdown files in a <code>command/</code> or <code>commands/</code> folder: the
            repository's <code>.vector/commands/</code>, <code>~/.vector/commands/</code>, or <code>commands/</code> in
            Vector's own config folder. Frontmatter carries the metadata, the body is the prompt template, and the
            file's path becomes the name, so <code>.vector/commands/review/api.md</code> is available as
            <code>review/api</code>. Type <code>/</code> and the name in the composer or the terminal, followed by the
            command's arguments, or run it with <code>vector run --command</code>. A repository's commands load once you
            trust it.
          </p>
          <p class="doc-cmd"><code>vector run --command review/api "the new billing endpoints"</code><button class="doc-cmd-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy this command">Copy</button></p>
          <h3 id="command-templates">Template syntax</h3>
          <p>Before the prompt is sent, Vector fills in the template:</p>
          <dl class="doc-rows">
            <div><dt>$ARGUMENTS</dt><dd>Everything typed after the command's name, as typed.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>$1, $2 …</dt><dd>The arguments one at a time. Arguments are separated by spaces, and a phrase in double or single quotes counts as one, without its quotes. The highest-numbered placeholder in the template takes its argument and every one after it, and a placeholder with no argument is left empty.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>No placeholder</dt><dd>A template with neither <code>$ARGUMENTS</code> nor a numbered placeholder gets the arguments added at its end, after a blank line.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>!`command`</dt><dd>Vector runs the shell command in the folder you work in and puts its output in place of the snippet. Inside a snippet, <code>$1</code> and <code>$ARGUMENTS</code> insert each argument as one quoted shell word, so an argument cannot become shell syntax; one the shell cannot quote safely stops the command with an error. Because the command is your own, from your settings or a repository you trust, its snippets run without asking; only a <a href="/docs/permissions">deny rule</a> stops one. Snippets in an MCP server's prompt or a skill ask like any other shell command.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>@path</dt><dd>Attaches that file or folder, with the path taken from the repository's root, or from your home folder when it starts with <code>~/</code>. When no such file exists and an agent has that name, Vector hands the request to that agent, as <code>@</code> and an agent's name does in the composer.</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <pre><code>{`---
description: Fix a GitHub issue by its number
---
Fix issue #$1. This is the issue:

!\`gh issue view $1\`

Follow @CONTRIBUTING.md, and run the tests before you finish.`}</code></pre>
          <h3 id="command-fields">Frontmatter</h3>
          <dl class="doc-rows">
            <div><dt>description</dt><dd>Shown beside the command in the list of commands.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>agent</dt><dd>The agent that runs the command. When it is a subagent, the command runs as a separate subagent task that reports back, unless <code>subtask</code> is <code>false</code>.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>subtask</dt><dd><code>true</code> runs the command as a subagent task, and <code>false</code> keeps it in the conversation.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>model</dt><dd>The model the command runs on, as <code>provider/model</code>. Without it, a command that names an agent uses that agent's model. A session on a free model keeps that model.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>size</dt><dd>How much harness the run gets: <code>chat</code>, <code>light</code>, <code>standard</code> or <code>thorough</code>. Without it the run is standard, unless the arguments ask for a thorough job; only thorough offers subagents.</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <p>
            The <code>command</code> key in <code>vector.json</code> takes the same fields, with the template in
            <code>template</code>. A command of your own named <code>init</code> or <code>review</code> replaces Vector's.
            Prompts from your MCP servers are listed as commands too, named <code>&lt;server&gt;:&lt;prompt&gt;</code>
            with their arguments as <code>$1</code>, <code>$2</code> and so on, and so is each skill, under its own
            name, unless a command already has that name.
          </p>
          <pre><code>{`{
  "command": {
    "changelog": {
      "description": "Draft release notes since a tag",
      "template": "Write release notes for the changes since $1:\\n\\n!\`git log --oneline $1..HEAD\`",
      "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5"
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
</DocsPage>
